artists didn't just take pictures  they made pictures and the piece that  you see here  by robert rauschenberg is titled fan and  man  3. many believe that  the polaroid film process itself  valued and encouraged invention and  experimentation  it was like a blank canvas on which  artists made their personal mark  consequently artists manipulated prints  with  inks and dyes and um  pinpricks and cutting and collaging and  all kinds of crazy things one  photographer that i knew  actually told me that he put  his sx70 photographs in a toaster oven  to see what would happen  so i chose robert rauschenberg  rauschenberg as you know it was a noted  painter  and a printmaker he was an abstract  expressionist  and he spent his creative life  experimenting in his art  uh rashenberg first used the polaroid  20x24 camera  in the 1980s and this is a huge camera  it's about five feet tall almost as tall  as i am  weighed about 250 pounds and they  put it in the back of a u-haul and they  drove around the streets of miami  he photographed a sides of walls  derelict buildings things like that that  he would then photograph  and document the positive was hung to  dry  now normally you would stabilize and  preserve the image  by coating the print with polaroid fixer  however rauschenberg would have nothing  to do with that he did not want to fix  the prints yet  he took them back to his home in captiva  island in florida  where he hung all the prints on the  studio walls  still without applying the fixer  this allowed them to oxidize change  color  to continually change with air and light  and rain and even using  water from his garden hose on some of  the photographs  so he watched he waited and when he saw  something  in a particular image that delighted him  then  he would get a big bucket of fixer and a  paintbrush and he would paint that  specific area  of the print if he didn't like the rest  of what was going on  he waited he did not fix it he did not  want at that time to  stop the oxidation from taking place  now over time eventually he painted  fixer on the total surface  of each one of the prints in order to  discontinue  the image disintegration the body of  this work is called bleachers  this print is adhered to aluminum sheet  and there is no glass or plexi covering  the surface of the print  so this in a very perverse way could  continue  the organic development of the image  should anyone  god forbid touch the prince surface  then you'd have fingerprints added to  this beautiful image so i hope this  gives you  a sense of a kind of artistic  interference or disruption that took  place to create  what i think is really a beautiful very  tactile  photograph  you
 

